 This is an absolute value problem. The general approach to solving an equation involving absolute values is to consider the cases where the expression inside the absolute value is positive or negative. Here, we distribute the absolute value by performing the two different cases:<Tags: think: thinking process}
\begin{align*}
x-3=2x+4\;\;\text{ or }\;\;-(x-3)&=2x+4          \\
x-3=2x+4\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;-x+3&=2x+4&&\blue{\text{ getting }0 \text{ in each side}}   \\
-x-7&=0\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;-3x&=1&\blue{\text{ adding  }7,\text{ adding }3x}   \\
x&=\boxed{-\frac{7}{3}}\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\frac{x}{3}&=\boxed{-\frac{1}{3}}&&\red{\text{ simplifying the left-rhs and right-rhs}}\end{align*}</Tags: think: thinking process> The final answer: $\boxed{x=-\frac{7}{3}\;\;,\;\;x=-\frac{1}{3}}$ 
